      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**This is a strong offer. I would negotiate once, but I would not walk away over compensation unless Google materially changes the terms or you have serious concerns about the role.**\n\nMy recommendation:\n\n1. **Counter once, enthusiastically and without bluffing.**\n2. Ask for:\n   - **$271,000 base**\n   - **$1.5 million in new-hire RSUs**\n   - **$250,000 sign-on**\n3. If Google holds firm, the current offer is financially attractive enough that I would likely accept\u2014assuming you prefer the role, manager, scope, and career path.\n\n---\n\n## What the offer is actually worth\n\n### Four-year value\n\nIgnoring stock-price movement, raises, refreshes, and bonus over/under-performance:\n\n| Component | Four-year value |\n|---|---:|\n| Base: $260K \u00d7 4 | $1,040,000 |\n| Target bonus: $52K \u00d7 4 | $208,000 |\n| New-hire RSUs | $1,340,000 |\n| Sign-on | $200,000 |\n| **Total** | **$2,788,000** |\n| **Four-year average** | **$697,000/year** |\n\nExcluding the one-time sign-on, your four-year average recurring compensation is approximately **$647,000 per year**.\n\nFor context, Levels.fyi currently reports approximately **$606,000 median annual compensation for Google L6 product managers in the Bay Area**, although this is self-reported data and mixes employees at different points in their grant and refresh cycles. Your offer is about 7% above that benchmark on a four-year recurring basis, and substantially above it when the sign-on is included. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area))\n\n### Likely year-by-year pattern\n\nGoogle\u2019s commonly reported new-hire GSU schedule is front-loaded at **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**, although your actual offer documents control and you should confirm this explicitly. ([thesimplemoney.com](https://thesimplemoney.com/employers/google/gsu-grants-and-vesting-schedule))\n\nIf that schedule applies:\n\n| Year | Base + target bonus | New-hire RSUs | Sign-on | Estimated TC |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | $312,000 | $509,200 | $200,000 | **$1,021,200** |\n| 2 | $312,000 | $428,800 | \u2014 | **$740,800** |\n| 3 | $312,000 | $268,000 | \u2014 | **$580,000** |\n| 4 | $312,000 | $134,000 | \u2014 | **$446,000** |\n\nThat makes the offer exceptionally strong in Years 1\u20132, competitive in Year 3, and potentially below your current Meta run rate in Year 4 if you receive no refreshes.\n\nThat Year 4 decline is the main economic risk\u2014not whether the initial offer is good.\n\n---\n\n## Comparison with staying at Meta\n\nYour stated Meta compensation:\n\n- Normal annual run rate: approximately **$526,000**\n- Next 12 months based on actual expected vesting: approximately **$476,000**\n  - $240K base\n  - $36K bonus\n  - $200K vesting RSUs\n\nOver four years, assuming Meta stayed around $526K and ignoring future raises, promotions, and refreshes, that is roughly **$2.10 million**.\n\nGoogle\u2019s offer is approximately **$2.79 million over four years**, or about **$684,000 more gross compensation** before considering:\n\n- Meta and Alphabet share-price changes\n- Future equity refreshes at either company\n- Compensation raises\n- Bonus performance\n- Promotion probability\n- Starting-date and bonus-proration effects\n\nThe Google sign-on also approximately replaces the **$200,000 of Meta stock you would forfeit over the next 12 months**. That is a sensible make-whole, not an obvious lowball.\n\n---\n\n## How good are the individual components?\n\n### Base: strong, with a little room\n\nAt $260K, you are only $11K below the posted maximum of $271K.\n\nThat is already a high base offer. But the cap gives you an obvious, defensible counter: **ask for $271K**. Because the bonus is 20% of base, moving to $271K adds:\n\n- $11K annual base\n- $2.2K annual target bonus\n- Approximately **$52,800 over four years**, before raises\n\n### Equity: strong but worth pushing\n\nThe $1.34 million grant annualizes to $335K over four years. Combined with base and target bonus, that gives you a $647K recurring four-year average.\n\nThis is good, but equity is probably where Google has the greatest ability to improve the offer. A counter at **$1.5 million** is reasonable:\n\n- Increase requested: **$160,000**\n- Annualized increase: $40,000\n- Only about a 12% increase to the original equity grant\n\nI would not make an extreme demand such as $2 million without competing offers or exceptional leverage.\n\n### Sign-on: already very good\n\nThe $200K sign-on roughly matches your next-12-month Meta vesting loss. You can still ask for **$250K**, but this should be your third priority.\n\nIf Google says the sign-on is already at its limit, that would not concern me. I would rather get:\n\n1. Higher base\n2. More equity\n3. Then a larger sign-on\n\n---\n\n## Exactly what I would ask for\n\nUse one clean package request:\n\n> I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team and the role, and Google is my first choice. After reviewing the package against my current compensation at Meta and the equity I would be leaving behind, I\u2019d like to see whether there is room to bring the offer to $271,000 in base salary, $1.5 million in new-hire equity, and a $250,000 sign-on bonus.  \n>  \n> If we can get closer to that package, I would feel very comfortable moving forward. My priorities are base salary first, followed by the equity grant.\n\nA slightly firmer version, if you are actually prepared to accept promptly:\n\n> Google is my first choice, and if the team can get the package to $271,000 base, $1.5 million in equity, and $250,000 sign-on, I\u2019d be prepared to sign promptly.\n\nOnly use the second sentence if it is true.\n\n### Important positioning\n\nDo:\n\n- Express strong enthusiasm.\n- Use your Meta compensation and forfeited equity as the rationale.\n- Say compensation is important because of the economic cost and risk of moving.\n- Make a specific counter.\n- Indicate that base and equity are priorities.\n\nDon\u2019t:\n\n- Invent another offer.\n- Claim the current offer is below market; the evidence does not support that.\n- Threaten to walk.\n- negotiate each component in separate rounds.\n- Say Google must \u201cbeat\u201d every unusually good Meta vesting year indefinitely.\n\n---\n\n## What outcome should you expect?\n\nA reasonable negotiation might produce one of these:\n\n- Base increased to **$271K**\n- Equity increased to **$1.45M\u2013$1.5M**\n- Sign-on increased by **$25K\u2013$50K**\n- Some combination of the above\n\nEven an equity-only increase from $1.34M to $1.5M would be a meaningful win.\n\nIf they improve only one component, my preference would be:\n\n1. **Base to $271K**, because it is recurring and increases target bonus\n2. **At least $100K\u2013$160K additional equity**\n3. **Additional sign-on**\n\nIf Google says the offer is final, I would not interpret that as evidence that you were lowballed.\n\n---\n\n## Don\u2019t rely on refreshes in deciding\n\nTreat future refresh grants as **upside, not guaranteed compensation**.\n\nGoogle does have an annual compensation process under which equity awards can be influenced by role, performance, location, manager judgment, and broader compensation decisions. But that does not make any specific refresh amount guaranteed. ([abc.xyz](https://abc.xyz/assets/a7/e7/b681d86643dc9f84e4a84bdac0ad/pay-equity-info-sheet.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nThe front-loaded initial grant means refreshes matter greatly by Years 3\u20134. On the reported 38/32/20/10 schedule, your initial stock compensation falls from about $509K in Year 1 to $134K in Year 4. Refresh grants reportedly vest separately and more evenly, which can partially fill that decline\u2014but you cannot safely assign them a dollar value before joining. ([thesimplemoney.com](https://thesimplemoney.com/employers/google/gsu-grants-and-vesting-schedule))\n\nI would evaluate the offer this way:\n\n- **Guaranteed four-year offer:** excellent\n- **Year 1\u20132 compensation:** exceptional\n- **Year 3:** solid\n- **Year 4 without refreshes:** weak relative to your current compensation\n- **Long-term compensation:** depends heavily on refreshes, performance and promotion\n\n---\n\n## Questions to confirm before signing\n\nGet these answered in writing:\n\n1. **What is the exact GSU vesting schedule?**\n   - Do not assume 38/32/20/10.\n2. **When is the grant date and first vest?**\n   - Google equity grants are often dated after the start month, so timing can affect your first-year cash flow. ([thesimplemoney.com](https://thesimplemoney.com/employers/google/gsu-grants-and-vesting-schedule))\n3. **Is the first annual bonus prorated based on start date?**\n4. **When is the $200K sign-on paid?**\n5. **What is the sign-on repayment clause?**\n   - Full repayment for leaving within one year?\n   - Prorated repayment?\n   - Separate treatment if terminated without cause?\n6. **Will you be eligible for the next compensation and refresh cycle?**\n7. **Can the recruiter provide historical\u2014not promised\u2014refresh ranges for successful L6 PMs on this team or within the organization?**\n8. **Does the grant specify a dollar value or fixed share count, and when is the conversion price determined?**\n\nAlso try to time your Meta departure immediately after any significant vesting date, provided that does not jeopardize the Google start date or offer.\n\n## Final recommendation\n\n**Negotiate, then accept unless the role itself gives you pause.**\n\nThe current package is already strong enough that walking away solely over compensation would be difficult to justify. You have meaningful leverage because staying at Meta is a credible and lucrative alternative, even though you do not have another external offer.\n\nMy precise counter would be:\n\n- **$271K base**\n- **$1.5M RSUs**\n- **$250K sign-on**\n\nIf they decline completely, I would still view **$260K / $1.34M / $200K** as a good offer\u2014particularly because Google is your first choice and the guaranteed four-year value materially exceeds your current run rate.",
